rtem (failure by 2031):\nMost likely failure paths:\n- Tryngolza dominates SHTG and REDEMPLO is confined to FCS \u2192 peak sales <$500M \u2192 fair value <$25.\n- Ionis wins a broad injunction or sizable royalty \u2192 REDEMPLO economics destroyed.\n- Obesity/neurology programs fail to reproduce early data \u2192 platform value collapses.\n- Cash burn forces 20-30% dilution \u2192 per-share value severely diluted.\n- REDEMPLO FCS reimbursement stalls due to a small prescriber base and high price \u2192 launch fizzles.\n\nEarly warning indicators: prescription flatlines, sNDA slippage, litigation loss, outside capital raise at a discount, SBC/share count acceleration.\n\nSECTION 9. RED TEAM & ADVERSARIAL ANALYSIS\n-------------------------------------------------------------\nShort thesis (using the same data):\n1. REDEMPLO product sales are still invisible inside total revenue; the company\u2019s TTM revenue includes fading Sarepta/commercial collaboration income. FY27 consensus revenue declines -26.6% [Aggregator].\n2. Ionis\u2019s Tryngolza is first in SHTG, already has $108M sales in 2025 and $27M in Q1 2026 [Estimate], and Ionis guides >$2B peak [Estimate]. REDEMPLO is second in a contest where the market is pricing REDEMPLO to win.\n3. The Ionis patent dispute (US 9,593,333) creates an injunction/royalty overhang [Estimate].\n4. FCS alone is ~6,500 US patients [Actual]; if SHTG is the real value, the launch is 12-24 months behind Tryngolza.\n5. SBC and dilution: 1-yr share growth +11.7%, 3-yr CAGR +7.7% [Arithmetic]; SBC ~$63-78M/yr [Filed].\n6. At $87.11 the market implies FY35 revenue of ~$5-8B (reverse DCF), more than 2\u00d7 our base case and even above Ionis\u2019s optimistic sHTG peak.\n7. Obesity data are Phase 1/2a; base rates of moving from Phase 1/2a to a commercial obesity drug are low.\n\nLogic combat:\n- Long logic strength: 5/10 (approved product, cash, platform, partnership validation)\n- Short logic strength: 7/10 (valuation, competition, litigation, dilution)\n- Gap = 2 \u2192 Conviction downgrade applied (per framework).\n\nAdversarial reference: no Hindenburg-style short-seller report was found [Unconfirmed]; adversarial cases are publicly outlined by Ionis\u2019s litigation claims [Estimate \u2014 FierceBiotech].\n\nKilled arguments (and when they resurrect):\n- \"ARWR is a platform company worth a baseline premium\": currently killed because no second approved asset exists; resurrects only if ARO-INHBE/ARO-ALK7 produce Phase 2b data with a partnership.\n- \"Sarepta milestones ($10B) are underappreciated\": killed until milestones are actually triggered; milestones remain in bull case only.\n- \"REDEMPLO's quarterly dosing will beat Tryngolza\": plausible but unproven; it matters only in SHTG, where Ionis already has data and access established.\n\nSECTION 10. AUDIT RESULTS\n-------------------------------------------------------------\nAudit performed across methodology, valuation, bias, consistency, narrative, red team, and data integrity. 37 points checked.\n- Pass: engine selection justified; no DCF-only conclusion; no peer-multiple-only conclusion; no TAM-only conclusion; macro considered; base rates used; SBC treated as real expense; no mixing of core and optionality; platform premium excluded from base; position sizing and monitoring KPIs provided; Red Team performed; Pre-mortem performed.\n- Unresolved data flags (not arithmetic failures):\n  - Macro yields and Fed stance could not be verified at T0 [Unconfirmed]\n  - Product/collaboration segment split is absent from the XBRL pack [Unconfirmed]\n  - Current total debt cannot be reconciled between vendor ($0.366B) and filed components (long-term debt not filed; leases $0.111B) [Unconfirmed]\n- Net result: PASS with caveats. Re-evaluation required if any unresolved flag becomes material.\n\nSECTION 11. DECISION JOURNAL ENTRY\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n- Date: 2026-08-24\n- T0 price: $87.11\n- Probability-wei